embly is holding a march at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n39,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,UNITY MARCH,The Unity March is taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n40,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,TRIBUNE SPOTLIGHT,Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity march taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza\n41,VERDANT OASIS PLAZA,BAILEY ASADI,Bailey Asadi is speaking at Verdant Oasis Plaza about the march\n43,HARMONY ASSEMBLY,UNITY MARCH,Harmony Assembly is organizing the Unity March\n\nOutput:\n{{\n    \"title\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza and Unity March\",\n    \"summary\": \"The community revolves around the Verdant Oasis Plaza, which is the location of the Unity March. The plaza has relationships with the Harmony Assembly, Unity March, and Tribune Spotlight, all of which are associated with the march event.\",\n    \"rating\": 5.0,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The impact severity rating is moderate due to the potential for unrest or conflict during the Unity March.\",\n    \"findings\": [\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza as the central location\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Verdant Oasis Plaza is the central entity in this community, serving as the location for the Unity March. This plaza is the common link between all other entities, suggesting its significance in the community. The plaza's association with the march could potentially lead to issues such as public disorder or conflict, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. [Data: Entities (5), Relationships (37, 38, 39, 40, 41,+more)]\"\n        }},\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Harmony Assembly's role in the community\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Harmony Assembly is another key entity in this community, being the organizer of the march at Verdant Oasis Plaza. The nature of Harmony Assembly and its march could be a potential source of threat, depending on their objectives and the reactions they provoke. The relationship between Harmony Assembly and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Entities(6), Relationships (38, 43)]\"\n        }},\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Unity March as a significant event\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The Unity March is a significant event taking place at Verdant Oasis Plaza. This event is a key factor in the community's dynamics and could be a potential source of threat, depending on the nature of the march and the reactions it provokes. The relationship between the march and the plaza is crucial in understanding the dynamics of this community. [Data: Relationships (39)]\"\n        }},\n        {{\n            \"summary\": \"Role of Tribune Spotlight\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Tribune Spotlight is reporting on the Unity March taking place in Verdant Oasis Plaza. This suggests that the event has attracted media attention, which could amplify its impact on the community. The role of Tribune Spotlight could be significant in shaping public perception of the event and the entities involved. [Data: Relationships (40)]\"\n        }}\n    ]\n}}\n\n\n# Real Data\n\nUse the following text for your answer. Do not make anything up in your answer.\n\nText:\n----Reports-----\ncommunity,full_content\r\n96,\"# Nutrition and Medical Care Community\n\nThe community is centered around key medical professionals and institutions specializing in nutrition, particularly in the context of various health conditions. The entities are interconnected through their contributions to medical literature and practices, focusing on nutritional requirements, artificial nutrition, and specific medical conditions such as cystic fibrosis and burn care.\n\n## M. Argente Pla's extensive contributions to nutrition literature\n\nM. Argente Pla is a prominent medical doctor with significant contributions to various chapters related to nutrition in medical contexts. His work includes topics such as nutrition and diabetes in cystic fibrosis, artificial nutrition in acute pancreatitis, and nutritional assessment. This extensive involvement highlights his expertise and the importance of his contributions to the field of nutrition, particularly in complex medical scenarios. His role as an author and contributor to educational materials at La Fe Hospital emphasizes the integration of his research into practical applications in patient care [Data: Entities (1683); Relationships (4594, 2621, 6778, 4282, 3681, +more)].\n\n## K. García Malpartida's focus on enteral and parenteral nutrition\n\nK. García Malpartida is a notable author who has made significant contributions to chapters on enteral and parenteral nutrition. Their work addresses complications related to these forms of nutrition and discusses their applications in various medical contexts, including diabetes and cystic fibrosis. This focus on enteral and parenteral nutrition is crucial for understanding the nutritional needs of patients who cannot meet their requirements through standard dietary means, thereby enhancing patient care and outcomes [Data: Entities (1684); Relationships (7177, 4283, 2604, 3682, +more)].\n\n## P. Justo Pavia's expertise in trauma and burn care\n\nP. Justo Pavia is a medical professional who has contributed significantly to the understanding of nutritional support in the context of polytraumatism and burn care. His work emphasizes the importance of tailored nutritional strategies for patients with severe injuries, highlighting the critical role of nutrition in recovery and rehabilitation. This expertise is vital for healthcare providers in managing complex cases where nutritional needs are heightened due to trauma [Data: Entities (2110); Relationships (3703)].\n\n## La Fe Hospital's comprehensive approach to nutritional care\n\nLa Fe Hospital Universitari i Politècnic serves as a central institution in this community, specializing in enteral and parenteral nutrition. The hospital's commitment to nutritional screening and assessment upon patient admission ensures that individual needs are met effectively. This comprehensive approach not only addresses immediate nutritional requirements but also contributes to long-term health outcomes for patients, particularly those with chronic conditions or complex medical needs [Data: Entities (1665); Relationships (922, 3690, 7150, +more)].\n\n## The role of the Comisión de Nutrición Artificial, Dietética y Dietoterapia\n\nThe Comisión de Nutrición Artificial, Dietética y Dietoterapia at La Fe Hospital plays a crucial role in guiding nutritional practices and protocols. This committee focuses on developing guidelines for artificial nutrition and dietary therapy, ensuring that healthcare providers adhere to best practices in patient care. Their work is essential for maintaining high standards in nutritional management, particularly for patients requiring specialized dietary interventions [Data: Entities (1594); Relationships (6675, 6676, 6677, +more)].\"\r\n103,\"# Central Venous Catheters and Associated Infections\n\nThe community focuses on the medical entities related to central venous catheters (CVCs), including their use, associated infections, and treatment protocols. Key entities such as alteplase, various bacteria, and diagnostic procedures are interconnected through their roles in managing complications arising from CVCs, particularly in critical care settings.\n\n## CVCs are critical in patient management but pose infection risks\n\nCentral venous catheters (CVCs) are essential for administering medications, fluids, and nutrition in patients, especially in critical care settings. However, their use is associated with a high risk of infections, including bacteremia and catheter-related infections. The relationship between CVCs and various pathogens, such as Staphylococcus aureus and Enterococcus, highlights the need for stringent infection control measures. CVCs are commonly used in intensive care units, where the risk of complications is heightened due to the patients' critical conditions [Data: Entities (2529, 2839, 2840, 2856, 12867); Relationships (10125, 10135, 10114, 10120, 10129)].\n\n## Alteplase is used to manage CVC occlusions\n\nAlteplase is a thrombolytic agent utilized to dissolve blood clots that can obstruct central venous catheters. Its administration is crucial in restoring the functionality of CVCs, particularly in patients experiencing thrombotic complications. The use of alteplase in conjunction with CVCs underscores the importance of timely intervention to prevent further complications, such as infections or prolonged catheter dysfunction [Data: Entities (2815); Relationships (10104)].\n\n## Infections related to CVCs are a major concern in healthcare\n\nInfections associated with central venous catheters, such as those caused by Staphylococcus aureus and Candida species, are significant health risks. These infections can lead to severe complications, including endocarditis and osteomyelitis, particularly in immunocompromised patients. The prevalence of these infections necessitates careful monitoring and management strategies to mitigate risks, especially in intensive care settings where CVCs are frequently used [Data: Entities (2839, 2853, 2856, 2840, 12867); Relationships (10122, 10123, 10116, 10119, 10128)].\n\n## Diagnostic procedures play a vital role in managing CVC complications\n\nProcedures such as echography and catheterography are essential for diagnosing complications related to central venous catheters. These diagnostic tools help identify thrombotic issues and assess the condition of the CVC, allowing for timely interventions. The integration of these diagnostic techniques into patient management protocols is crucial for preventing severe outcomes associated with CVC use [Data: Entities (2820, 2819); Relationships (10109, 10662)].\n\n## Antibiotic therapy is critical for managing CVC-related infections\n\nAntibiotic therapy is often required to treat infections that arise from the use of central venous catheters. The choice of antibiotics may be tailored based on culture results to ensure effective management of the infection. This highlights the importance of prompt diagnosis and treatment in preventing the escalation of infections associated with CVCs [Data: Entities (2857); Relationships (10120, 1965)].\"\r\n99,\"# Infection Risks Associated with Central Venous Catheters\n\nThis community focuses on the health risks associated with infections related to the use of central venous catheters (CVCs). Key entities include various medical conditions and complications that arise from catheter use, highlighting the interconnectedness of these risks and the importance of proper medical practices.\n\n## Infection as a primary complication of CVCs\n\nInfections are the most common complication associated with the use of central venous catheters (CVCs). These infections can lead to severe health issues, including sepsis, particularly in patients who are already immunocompromised or critically ill. The relationship between CVCs and infections underscores the need for stringent hygiene practices and monitoring in clinical settings. [Data: Entities (13241, 12518); Relationships (10135, 19178, 7807)]\n\n## Vulnerability of immunocompromised patients\n\nPatients who are immunosuppressed, such as those who have undergone hematopoietic transplants, are at a heightened risk for infections related to CVCs. This vulnerability necessitates careful management and monitoring to prevent complications that could arise from catheter use. The relationship between immunosuppression and infection risk is critical in understanding patient care strategies. [Data: Entities (13241); Relationships (3485)]\n\n## Complications arising from catheter insertion\n\nThe insertion of central venous catheters can lead to various complications, including pneumothorax, hemothorax, and gas embolism. These complications can significantly impact patient outcomes and highlight the importance of skilled personnel during catheter placement. Understanding these risks is essential for healthcare providers to minimize adverse events. [Data: Entities (2059, 2060, 14876); Relationships (8764, 8766, 19179)]\n\n## Role of parenteral nutrition in infection risk\n\nCentral venous catheters are often required for administering high-osmolarity parenteral nutrition, which is critical for patients unable to eat. However, this necessity also increases the risk of infections, making it vital for healthcare providers to balance nutritional needs with the potential for complications. The relationship between parenteral nutrition and CVCs is a key consideration in patient management. [Data: Entities (12518); Relationships (4888)]\n\n## Importance of hygiene in infection prevention\n\nPatient hygiene is a significant factor influencing the risk of infection in individuals with central venous catheters. Poor hygiene practices can lead to increased susceptibility to infections, emphasizing the need for education and adherence to hygiene protocols in clinical settings. This relationship highlights the multifaceted approach required to mitigate infection risks. [Data: Entities (14874); Relationships (19424, 5207)]\n\n## Impact of experienced personnel on complication rates\n\nThe involvement of experienced personnel in the insertion of central venous catheters can significantly reduce the risk of complications. Skilled practitioners are better equipped to handle the technical challenges associated with catheter placement, thereby minimizing the likelihood of adverse events. This relationship underscores the importance of training and expertise in healthcare settings. [Data: Entities (14880); Relationships (19180)]\"\r\n102,\"# Critical Care Nutrition and Thrombosis Management\n\nThis community encompasses key medical entities involved in the management of critically ill patients, particularly focusing on conditions such as Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (SDRA), nutritional support through artificial means, and the complications associated with thrombosis. The relationships among these entities highlight the importance of integrated care in intensive settings.\n\n## SDRA and its nutritional management\n\nSDRA, or Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome, is a severe condition that necessitates specialized nutritional support for recovery. The relationship between SDRA and artificial nutrition is crucial, as enteral nutrition can significantly improve patient outcomes. Nutritional management is critical for patients with SDRA to support recovery and improve outcomes [Data: Entities (2163, 2164); Relationships (2838, 9063)].\n\n## Role of artificial nutrition in critical care\n\nArtificial nutrition is vital for critically ill patients, particularly those with conditions like inflammatory bowel disease and COPD. It aims to enhance nutritional intake and assist in the remission of diseases, thereby improving overall health. The use of artificial nutrition is a common practice in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU) to ensure that patients receive adequate nutrients during their recovery [Data: Entities (2164); Relationships (6948, 9031, 9061)].\n\n## Thrombosis as a complication in critical care\n\nThrombosis is a significant concern for critically ill patients, especially those receiving central nutrition. The risk of thrombosis can complicate medical interventions and pose serious health risks. Understanding the relationship between thrombosis and venous access procedures is essential for effective patient management [Data: Entities (2835); Relationships (10678, 10679, 10680)].\n\n## Importance of venous access procedures\n\nVenous access is a critical component of treatment in the ICU, allowing for the administration of medications and fluids. However, it is associated with risks such as infection and thrombosis. The relationship between venous access and various medical conditions highlights the need for careful management to minimize complications [Data: Entities (14857); Relationships (13343, 9062, 19951)].\n\n## Anatomy's role in medical interventions\n\nA thorough understanding of anatomy is essential for effective medical interventions, particularly in the context of venous access and managing conditions like Short Bowel Syndrome (SIC). Knowledge of anatomical structures aids in determining suitable sites for venous access, thereby reducing the risk of complications [Data: Entities (12483); Relationships (14353, 19155)].\"\r\n97,\"# Inflammatory Bowel Disease Community\n\nThe community focuses on the management and research of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD), including Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis. Key entities include various dietary therapies, medical journals, and professional organizations that contribute to the understanding and treatment of these conditions.\n\n## Diet Therapy's Role in Managing IBD\n\nDiet therapy is essential for managing health conditions like Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD). It aims to improve the nutritional status of patients, which is crucial given the malabsorption issues associated with IBD. The relationship between diet therapy and IBD highlights the importance of tailored nutritional interventions to enhance patient outcomes. [Data: Entities (10049); Relationships (4147, 17501)]\n\n## Understanding Inflammatory Bowel Disease\n\nInflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD) encompasses conditions like Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis, characterized by chronic inflammation of the gastrointestinal tract. The complexity of IBD necessitates a multifaceted approach to treatment, including dietary management and medical therapy. The interplay between gut microbiota and IBD progression underscores the need for ongoing research in this area. [Data: Entities (9228, 2250, 2251); Relationships (9430, 9434)]\n\n## Crohn's Disease and Its Nutritional Challenges\n\nCrohn's disease is a specific type of IBD that can lead to significant complications, including malabsorption of essential nutrients. Nutritional management is critical for patients with Crohn's disease, as it requires specific dietary considerations to help manage symptoms and maintain overall health. The relationship between Crohn's disease and diet therapy is vital for effective patient care. [Data: Entities (2250, 10049); Relationships (9433, 9429)]\n\n## The Role of Medical Journals in IBD Research\n\nJournals like CMAJ and J Nutr Health Aging publish significant research related to IBD, contributing to the advancement of medical knowledge and practice. These publications cover various aspects of IBD, including clinical nutrition and management strategies, which are essential for healthcare professionals. The dissemination of research findings through these journals plays a crucial role in improving patient care. [Data: Entities (12412, 12761); Relationships (2121, 19134)]\n\n## Professional Organizations and Guidelines\n\nOrganizations like the American Gastroenterological Association provide guidelines and research related to the management of IBD. These guidelines are essential for healthcare providers to ensure evidence-based practices in treating patients with Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis. The collaboration between professional organizations and clinical researchers enhances the quality of care for patients. [Data: Entities (2248, 9425); Relationships (9426, 17503)]\"\r\n104,\"# Catheter-Related Complications and Management\n\nThis community focuses on the medical implications of catheter use, particularly in relation to nutrition parenteral and the associated risks of venous thrombosis. Key entities include catheters, reflux gastroesophageal (RGE), and various complications arising from their use, highlighting the interconnectedness of these medical conditions and treatments.\n\n## Catheters as essential medical devices\n\nCatheters are critical for administering parenteral nutrition directly into the bloodstream, especially for patients with conditions like short bowel syndrome. However, their use is not without risks, as they can lead to severe complications such as sepsis and venous thrombosis. The relationship between catheters and nutrition parenteral underscores their importance in medical treatment, while also highlighting the need for vigilant monitoring to prevent adverse outcomes. [Data: Entities (517, 3659); Relationships (3659)]\n\n## Venous thrombosis as a significant complication\n\nVenous thrombosis is a serious complication that can arise from the use of central venous catheters (CVCs). This condition involves the formation of blood clots in the veins, which can lead to significant clinical issues, including the potential loss of venous access. The relationship between CVCs and thrombosis emphasizes the critical need for monitoring and management strategies to mitigate these risks, particularly in patients requiring long-term catheter use. [Data: Entities (2823); Relationships (10112, 3660)]\n\n## The role of pH-impedance testing in diagnosis\n\npH-impedance testing is a diagnostic technique that measures both pH and impedance levels in the esophagus, helping to detect reflux episodes, particularly in patients with RGE. This testing is crucial for correlating symptoms with reflux events, thereby guiding treatment decisions. The relationship between pH-impedance testing and RGE highlights the importance of accurate diagnosis in managing reflux-related conditions. [Data: Entities (512); Relationships (3644, 3656)]\n\n## Anticoagulants and their role in managing thrombosis\n\nAnticoagulants are medications used to treat venous thrombosis, a complication that can arise from catheter use. Their role is vital in preventing the progression of thrombosis and managing the associated risks. The relationship between anticoagulants and thrombosis underscores the importance of timely intervention in patients with catheters to prevent severe complications. [Data: Entities (12522); Relationships (10668)]\n\n## The impact of reflux gastroesophageal (RGE)\n\nRGE is a condition where stomach contents flow back into the esophagus, leading to symptoms such as heartburn and difficulty swallowing. This condition can complicate the management of patients with catheters, particularly those receiving parenteral nutrition. The relationship between RGE and its symptoms highlights the need for comprehensive care strategies that address both reflux and catheter-related complications. [Data: Entities (511); Relationships (147, 3657)]\"\r\n\n\n-----Entities-----\nhuman_readable_id,title,description,degree\r\n175,NEUMONÍA POR ASPIRACIÓN,\"NEUMONÍA POR ASPIRACIÓN, also known as aspiration pneumonia, is a lung infection that arises from the inhalation of saliva, food, liquid, or vomit into the lungs. This condition is often linked to dysphagia, which is difficulty swallowing, and can occur as a potential complication in patients receiving nasogastric feeding. The infection is characterized by the introduction of contaminated substances into the respiratory tract, leading to inflammation and infection in the lungs.\",10\r\n2353,GASTO ENERGÉTICO EN REPOSO,\"The \"\"GASTO ENERGÉTICO EN REPOSO\"\" refers to the amount of energy that the body utilizes while at rest. This expenditure can vary among individuals, particularly in patients with specific health conditions such as cancer. The resting energy expenditure (GER) is essential for understanding the energy requirements of the body in a resting state and can be calculated using specific formulas. This calculation is particularly important for determining the energy needs of patients, including those with conditions like cystic fibrosis, where accurate assessment of energy expenditure is crucial for effective management and treatment.\",3\r\n2528,NUTRICIÓN PARENTERAL CENTRAL,Nutritional therapy that accesses circulation through a large-caliber vein using a central venous catheter,4\r\n2052,HICKMAN,\"Hickman is a type of tunneled central venous catheter designed for accessing the venous system, particularly beneficial for patients requiring long-term intravenous therapy, such as those undergoing oncology treatments and other medical interventions. This catheter is known for its lower infection rates compared to non-tunneled catheters, making it a preferred choice for sustained venous access.\",3\r\n2531,GROSHONG,\"GROSHONG refers to a type of central venous catheter specifically designed for long-term venous access. This catheter is engineered to minimize the risk of infection and thrombosis, making it a preferred choice for patients requiring extended intravenous therapy.\",2\r\n2053,PORT-A-CATH,\"Port-a-Cath is a brand of totally implanted catheter designed for long-term access to the vascular system, primarily for patients who require frequent blood draws or infusions. It is classified as a type of central venous catheter, which necessitates regular maintenance, including sealing every 1 to 6 months to prevent thrombotic obstruction. This device provides a reliable solution for patients needing ongoing vascular access.\",2\r\n182,ANTIBIOTICOTERAPIA,\"Antibiotic therapy, also known as \"\"ANTIBIOTICOTERAPIA,\"\" involves the use of antibiotics to treat bacterial infections. This treatment is essential for managing infections that may arise from medical devices such as catheters. However, antibiotic therapy can also have significant effects on the oral flora, potentially increasing the risk of aspiration pneumonia, particularly in patients who experience dysphagia. Thus, while antibiotic therapy plays a critical role in combating bacterial infections, it is important to consider its implications on oral health and the associated risks in vulnerable populations.\",2\r\n178,KLEBSIELLA PNEUMONIAE,\"Klebsiella pneumoniae is a bacterium belonging to the Enterobacteriaceae family, known for its association with various infections, particularly catheter-related infections. This pathogen is also capable of causing aspiration pneumonia, especially in vulnerable populations such as the elderly or those with compromised immune systems. Its ability to thrive in hospital settings makes it a significant concern in healthcare-associated infections.\",2\r\n3253,PARAMETROS ANTROPOMETRICOS,\"PARAMETROS ANTROPOMETRICOS refer to anthropometric parameters, which are measurements utilized to assess the body composition and nutritional status of patients. These parameters play a crucial role in evaluating an individual's health and can provide valuable insights into their physical condition.\",2\r\n2249,M.D. HIGÓN BALLESTER,\"M.D. Higón Ballester is a medical doctor recognized for his contributions to the field of nutrition, particularly in relation to inflammatory bowel disease and cystic fibrosis. He has played a significant role in advancing knowledge and practices surrounding nutritional care for patients with these conditions, demonstrating a commitment to improving health outcomes through specialized dietary interventions.\",3\r\n2349,VALORACION NUTRICIONAL,\"VALORACION NUTRICIONAL refers to the comprehensive process of assessing an individual's nutritional status, which is essential for identifying and managing various health conditions. This assessment is particularly important in specific populations, such as patients with cystic fibrosis, where it involves evaluating dietary intake, clinical status, and biochemical markers to monitor overall health. Additionally, nutritional assessment plays a crucial role in the care of frail elderly individuals, helping to evaluate their nutritional needs and status. Overall, VALORACION NUTRICIONAL is vital for addressing issues like sarcopenia and frailty, ensuring that appropriate interventions can be implemented to improve health outcomes.\",9\r\n2808,CENTRAL VENOUS CATHETERS,\"Central venous catheters are medical devices designed to be inserted into a large vein, providing long-term access to the bloodstream for various medical treatments. They are particularly useful for long-term intravenous access, allowing for the administration of medications, fluids, and nutrients. However, it is important to note that the use of central venous catheters can lead to complications, especially in pediatric patients.\",8\r\n2346,FIBROSIS QUISTICA,\"Fibrosis quística (Cystic fibrosis) is a genetic disorder that affects the lungs and digestive system, requiring careful nutritional management.\",8\r\n176,HAEMOPHILUS INFLUENZAE,\"Haemophilus influenzae is a bacterium commonly associated with aspiration pneumonia, particularly in patients with dysphagia.\",1\r\n183,HIGIENE DENTAL,\"Dental hygiene is a factor that can affect the risk of aspiration pneumonia, particularly in patients with dysphagia.\",1\r\n177,STREPTOCOCCUS PNEUMONIAE,\"Streptococcus pneumoniae is another bacterium linked to aspiration pneumonia, often found in patients with swallowing difficulties.\",1\r\n2355,DENSITOMETRIA,\"Densitometry is a method used to assess bone density and body composition, important in the nutritional evaluation of cystic fibrosis patients.\",1\r\n12704,INTERVENCION NUTRICIONAL,\"Intervención nutricional refers to dietary interventions aimed at improving nutritional status, particularly in the elderly to combat frailty and sarcopenia.\",1\r\n3254,PARAMETROS BIOQUIMICOS,Biochemical parameters are laboratory tests used to evaluate the nutritional status and health of patients.,1\r\n2351,VALORACION CLÍNICA Y ANTROPOMÉTRICA,Clinical and anthropometric assessment is performed every three months to monitor growth and nutritional status in cystic fibrosis patients.,1\r\n2352,VALORACION HEMATOLÓGICA Y BIOQUÍMICA,Hematological and biochemical assessment is conducted annually to evaluate various health markers in cystic fibrosis patients.,1\r\n2347,NUTRICION EN FIBROSIS QUISTICA,Nutrition in cystic fibrosis focuses on maintaining adequate nutritional status to improve disease course and quality of life.,1\r\n2354,FUNCION PULMONAR,\"Pulmonary function is assessed to determine the severity of respiratory issues in cystic fibrosis patients, influencing nutritional needs.\",1\r\n2812,CATHETER OCLUSION,\"Catheter occlusion is a blockage that prevents the normal flow of fluids through a catheter, often requiring medical intervention.\",1\r\n2809,OBSTRUCTION OF CENTRAL VENOUS CATHETERS,\"Obstruction of central venous catheters is a complication that can occur in 14-36% of cases, affecting their functionality.\",1\r\n2811,THROMBOSIS,\"Thrombosis refers to the formation of a blood clot inside a blood vessel, which can complicate the use of central venous catheters in pediatric patients.\",1\r\n14856,SUBCUTANEOUS RESERVOIR,\"A subcutaneous reservoir is a device implanted under the skin to provide access to the venous system for medication delivery, often used in conjunction with catheters.\",1\r\n998,FOOD AND DRUG ADMINISTRATION,\"The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is a United States government agency tasked with the regulation of food, drugs, and medical devices. This federal agency plays a crucial role in ensuring the safety and efficacy of various products, including the approval of medical devices such as the VitalStim® for the treatment of dysphagia. Additionally, the FDA establishes guidelines for the use of medical devices, including catheters, in medical procedures, thereby overseeing a wide range of health-related products and practices to protect public health.\",3\r\n999,VITALSTIM®,\"VitalStim® is a device approved by the FDA for neuromuscular electrical stimulation to treat dysphagia, particularly effective in patients with swallowing difficulties.\",2\r\n2533,ANESTESIA Y REANIMACIÓN,Department responsible for ensuring the placement and management of catheters,1\r\n2534,RADIOLOGÍA PEDIÁTRICA INTERVENCIONISTA,Interventional pediatric radiology department involved in catheter placement,1\r\n1010,CHATTANOOGA GROUP,Chattanooga Group is the company that developed the VitalStim® device for treating dysphagia through neuromuscular e